Use `@testsnippet` for values and imports that each item should have directly in scope; use `@testmodule` for a namespace of helpers that items refer to by qualified name.

```julia
# partial
@testsnippet Fixtures begin
    xs = collect(1.0:5.0)          # `xs` is defined inside each item that lists Fixtures
end

@testmodule Helpers begin
    const K = 42
    double(x) = 2x
end

@testitem "uses both" setup=[Fixtures, Helpers] begin
    @test length(xs) == 5
    @test Helpers.double(Helpers.K) == 84   # module members need the qualifier
end
```

I ran the snippet case (the snippet's variable is visible unqualified) and the module case (`Shared.K` with the qualifier). In a `Pkg.test()` run where three items listed both, the module's body ran once and the snippet's body ran three times, so put expensive setup in the module. Either way the item must name the setup in `setup=[...]`; an unlisted setup is not available.

Review my test file. Why would these break when run one at a time?

```julia
# partial
@testitem "build" begin
    global cfg = Dict("n" => 3)
    @test cfg["n"] == 3
end

@testitem "use" begin
    @test cfg["n"] == 3
end
```

The second item depends on the first one having run. Items run in separate modules with no guaranteed order, so `use` raises `UndefVarError: cfg` whenever `build` has not run in the same module, which is always. Make each item self-contained and share setup explicitly:

```julia
# partial
@testsnippet Cfg begin
    cfg = Dict("n" => 3)
end

@testitem "build" setup=[Cfg] begin
    @test cfg["n"] == 3
end

@testitem "use" setup=[Cfg] begin
    @test cfg["n"] == 3
end
```

Self-containment is what makes filtering, parallel workers, and `julia_rerun_failed` valid. A suite whose items only pass in full-suite order cannot be iterated one item at a time.

Each item runs in its own module, so every item that includes the file evaluates it again from scratch, and the helpers end up duplicated across items and tied to a file path. Prefer a `@testmodule` or `@testsnippet` for helpers shared by several items; they are declared once and referenced by name via `setup=[...]`.

```julia
# partial
@testmodule Helpers begin
    approx_all(a, b; atol=1e-8) = all(x -> abs(x[1] - x[2]) <= atol, zip(a, b))
end

@testitem "uses helper" setup=[Helpers] begin
    @test Helpers.approx_all([1.0, 2.0], [1.0, 2.0 + 1e-10])
end
```

Give `@test_throws` a string or regex as the first argument; it then checks the message too (Julia 1.8 and later). A bare exception type only proves that something of that type was thrown.

```julia
using Test

f(x) = x < 0 ? throw(ArgumentError("x must be nonnegative, got $x")) : x

@test_throws "must be nonnegative" f(-1)       # substring of the message
@test_throws r"got -\d" f(-1)                    # regex
@test_throws ArgumentError("x must be nonnegative, got -1") f(-1)   # exact exception value
```

All three pass. A wrong message fails loudly, so the test cannot pass for the wrong reason: `@test_throws "other message" f(-1)` reports `Expected: "other message"` against `Message: "ArgumentError: x must be nonnegative, got -1"`. Test the type too when the type is part of the contract.

Run the same assertions against wrapped inputs, and assert that the output axes follow the input axes. An `AbstractVector` annotation promises the function works for any vector: `OffsetArray`, views, and so on.

```julia
# partial
@testitem "generic over array types" begin
    using OffsetArrays
    cumulative(A::AbstractVector) = (out = similar(A); acc = zero(eltype(A));
                                      for i in eachindex(A); acc += A[i]; out[i] = acc; end; out)
    base = [1, 2, 3, 4]
    expected = cumsum(base)
    for A in (base,
              OffsetArray(base, -2:1),
              view(vcat(0, base, 0), 2:5))
        r = cumulative(A)
        @test axes(r) == axes(A)
        @test collect(r) == expected
    end
end
```

Include at least one array whose indices do not start at 1. Without it, `1:length(A)` bugs pass every test.

Why does this copy fail on an OffsetArray?

```julia
copy_vec(A::AbstractVector) = (out = Vector{eltype(A)}(undef, length(A));
                               for i in eachindex(A); out[i] = A[i]; end; out)
```

A test item that fails on the old loop: it needs an input whose first index is not 1.

```julia
# partial
@testitem "running_total: non-1-based axes" begin
    using OffsetArrays
    A = OffsetArray([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], -2:0)
    r = running_total(A)
    @test axes(r) == axes(A)
    @test collect(r) == [1.0, 3.0, 6.0]
    @test collect(running_total(view([9.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 2:4))) == [1.0, 3.0, 6.0]
end
```

Check that the test fails when you restore `1:length(A)` before you rely on it: a regression test that never failed proves nothing. Also add `OffsetArrays` to `test/Project.toml`.

Not if the signature says `AbstractMatrix`. Add the cases that break common assumptions: a view (`view(A, 2:4, 2:4)`), a transposed or adjoint wrapper, a non-square shape if the function allows it, `Float32` as well as `Float64`, and an `OffsetArray` if you promise arbitrary axes. Loop over the cases with the same assertion on each, comparing against the result for `collect(B)`:

```julia
# partial
@testitem "generic matrix input" begin
    using OffsetArrays
    A = reshape(collect(1.0:16.0), 4, 4)
    for B in (A, view(A, 2:4, 1:3), A', OffsetArray(A, 0:3, 0:3))
        @test sum(B) == sum(collect(B))
        @test axes(B .+ 1) == axes(B)
    end
end
```
